+## Stock engine cvars whose ReCS default differs (2026-09-09)
+
+These three are stock CRYENGINE cvars, registered in `RendererCVars.cpp` and unchanged in
+behaviour. Only their **defaults** are ReCS policy: screen-space sun shafts are retired in favour
+of volumetric fog's real light shafts, and the fog is given the resolution and shadow reach it
+needs to stand in for them. This is a deliberate exception to the byte-identity rule, and a
+narrow one: the rule promises that the stock path's *code* is untouched, and it is - anyone who
+sets the stock values back (`r_sunshafts 2`, `r_VolumetricFogDownscaledSunShadow 1`,
+`r_VolumetricFogTexScale 10`) gets the stock frame byte for byte. The sun-shafts stage, its
+scene-referred atmospheric condition (`r_ShaftAtmosphereCondition`) and its composite all remain
+in the build for that case.
+
+### r_sunshafts
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| ReCS default | **0** (off) |
+| Stock 5.7.1 default | 2 (on, with occlusion query) |
+| DLL | CryRenderD3D11 |
+| Purpose | Master switch for the **screen-space** sun shafts (`CSunShaftsStage` and its composite in `HDRFinalPass`). The Time of Day's `Sun shafts visibility` / `Sun rays visibility` only publish `SunShafts_Active`, which the renderer ANDs with this cvar, so a level cannot force the shafts on past it. 1 = on, 2 = on with the sun-disc occlusion query. |
+
+Why retired: a screen-space shaft is a radial blur of bright pixels with no scattering term, no
+occluder test and no path length; every bound and condition the scene-referred work added to it
+(`decisions/s2-sun-shafts.md`, `decisions/s8-sun-shafts-condition.md`) was a patch on an
+approximation. Volumetric fog computes the same single-scattering integral term for term, shadow
+map included, and once S8 put it on scene units it became the correct source of god rays.
+
+### r_VolumetricFogDownscaledSunShadow
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| ReCS default | **2** |
+| Stock 5.7.1 default | 1 |
+| DLL | CryRenderD3D11 |
+| Purpose | How many sun shadow cascades the fog samples through its own downscaled copies. 0 = none (the fog reads the full-resolution maps; no cached static shadow), 1 = cascades 1-2 downscaled and the rest from the static shadow map if `r_ShadowsCache` allows, **2 = cascades 1-3 downscaled**. With 2 the third cascade - the distance band where an exterior's light shafts actually form - is shadowed inside the fog instead of falling back to the coarse static map, which is what makes the fog's shafts crisp enough to replace the screen-space ones. Cost: one more downscaled shadow map per frame (ratio from `r_VolumetricFogDownscaledSunShadowRatio`, default 1/8). |
+
+### r_VolumetricFogTexScale
+
+| | |
+| --- | --- |
+| ReCS default | **6** |
+| Stock 5.7.1 default | 10 |
+| DLL | CryRenderD3D11 |
+| Purpose | Divisor from screen resolution to the froxel grid's width and height (minimum 2; depth is `r_VolumetricFogTexDepth`, 32). 6 instead of 10 gives (10/6)^2 = **2.8x the froxels** - at 1080p a 320x180x32 volume instead of 192x108x32 - so shaft edges stop smearing across the sky and the temporal reprojection has less to hide. Cost scales with the froxel count in every fog pass (injection, in-scattering, integration): budget about 2.8x the stock fog time, which on the target hardware is still well under a millisecond. Set 10 back if the fog is the frame's bottleneck. |
+
+**How to get god rays on ReCS now.** `e_VolumetricFog 1` (Cry3DEngine, default still 0,
+`VF_REQUIRE_APP_RESTART` - set it in the project's `system.cfg` or `game.cfg` and restart the
+editor; it cannot be toggled live). Density comes from the Time of Day's volumetric fog `Global
+density` - the table in `SceneReferredCalibration.md` section 3.1 turns it into a visibility, and
+0.1 (haze, 3.9 km) to 0.4 (mist, 1 km) is where shafts read clearly. The two cvars above are
+already at their ReCS defaults. `e_VolumetricFog` itself is not changed by ReCS: it needs a restart,
+it changes which fog system every level's authoring is read through, and it is the user's call per
+project.
+
